// @check
// @error-at 40:4 cannot assign i64 to i32 — `s` on line 38 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `i32`; annotate it: `let s: i64 = …`
// @error-at 49:6 cannot assign i64 to i32 — `a` on line 49 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `i32`; annotate it: `let a: i64 = …`
// @error-at 51:2 cannot assign i64 to i32 — `b` on line 51 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `i32`; annotate it: `let b: i64 = …`
// @error-at 53:2 operator '*' mixes i32 and f32 — `c` on line 53 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `i32`; annotate it: `let c: f32 = …`
// @error-at 55:2 cannot assign f64 to i32 — `d` on line 55 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `i32`; annotate it: `let d: f64 = …`
// @error-at 63:8 cannot assign i64 to i32 — field `x` of `r` on line 63 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `i32`; write it `x: 0 as i64`
// @error-at 65:2 cannot assign i64 to i32 — `xs` on line 65 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `i32[]`; annotate it: `const xs: i64[] = …`
// @error-at 67:7 cannot assign u8[] to i32[] — `bs` on line 67 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `i32[]`; annotate it: `let bs: u8[] = …`
// @error-at 69:7 f32[] is not f64[]: a container never widens implicitly, and only a readonly list is covariant. Its elements would be stored differently, so it would be a copy, and a write through either list would not reach the other. Copy it with `.map((x) => x as f64)` — `fs` on line 69 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `f64[]`; annotate it: `let fs: f32[] = …`
// @error-at 77:25 cannot assign i64 to i32 — `total` on line 77 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `i32`; annotate it: `export let total: i64 = …`
// @error-at 79:0 operator '*' mixes i32 and f32 — `scale` on line 79 has no annotation, so its literal defaulted to `i32`; annotate it: `let scale: f32 = …`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `e` is inferred as `i32`
// @error-at 85:6 cannot assign i64 to i32
// @error-at 87:6 cannot assign i64 to i32
// @error-at 89:8 cannot assign i64 to i32
// @error-at 90:8 cannot assign f32 to i32
// @error-at 92:9 cannot assign u8[] to i32[]
// @error-at 94:9 cannot assign i64 to f64
